{"model":"kimi-k3","created_at":"2026-09-08T21:51:46.634833277Z","response":"{\"reference_patch\":\"def summarize_window(events):\\n    counts = {}\\n    peak = None\\n    total = 0\\n    unmuted = []\\n    seen = []\\n    for event in events:\\n        total += 1\\n        if event.get(\\\"muted\\\"):\\n            continue\\n        unmuted.append(event)\\n        label = classify_event(event)\\n        counts[label] = counts.get(label, 0) + 1\\n        if event.get(\\\"kind\\\") == \\\"reading\\\":\\n            value = event.get(\\\"value\\\")\\n            if value is not None and (peak is None or value \u003e peak):\\n                peak = value\\n        kind = event.get(\\\"kind\\\")\\n        if isinstance(kind, str) and kind not in seen:\\n            seen.append(kind)\\n    status = \\\"steady\\\"\\n    if counts.get(\\\"critical\\\"):\\n        status = \\\"alert\\\"\\n    elif counts.get(\\\"warning\\\") or counts.get(\\\"high\\\"):\\n        status = \\\"watch\\\"\\n    return {\\\"total\\\": total, \\\"counts\\\": counts, \\\"peak\\\": peak, \\\"status\\\": status, \\\"trend\\\": trend_of(unmuted), \\\"seen_kinds\\\": sorted(seen)}\",\"public_checks\":[{\"check_id\":\"r1pub1\",\"symbol\":\"summarize_window\",\"input\":[[{\"kind\":\"reading\",\"value\":10,\"ts\":1},{\"kind\":\"reading\",\"value\":30,\"ts\":2}]],\"expected_values\":[{\"total\":2,\"counts\":{\"low\":1,\"normal\":1},\"peak\":30,\"status\":\"steady\",\"trend\":\"rising\",\"seen_kinds\":[\"reading\"]}],\"active_from_round\":1,\"active_through_round\":2,\"source_ids\":[\"m01\",\"m05\"],\"behavior\":\"functionality\",\"rationale\":\"m01 requires a new 'trend' key equal to trend_of(events) while total, counts, peak and status keep working exactly as before; readings 10 then 30 give delta 20 so the unchanged trend_of returns 'rising', and counts/peak/status follow the pre-existing aggregation rules.\"},{\"check_id\":\"r1pub2\",\"symbol\":\"summarize_window\",\"input\":[[{\"kind\":\"alarm\",\"value\":95,\"muted\":true},{\"kind\":\"reading\",\"value\":60,\"ts\":2},{\"kind\":\"heartbeat\"}]],\"expected_values\":[{\"total\":3,\"counts\":{\"normal\":1,\"ok\":1},\"peak\":60,\"status\":\"steady\",\"trend\":\"flat\",\"seen_kinds\":[\"heartbeat\",\"reading\"]}],\"active_from_round\":1,\"active_through_round\":2,\"source_ids\":[\"m01\",\"m02\",\"m03\",\"m05\"],\"behavior\":\"functionality\",\"rationale\":\"m02 keeps the muted 95-alarm in total but excludes it from counts, peak, status and trend; m03 adds sorted distinct seen_kinds of unmuted events; with a single unmuted reading value the unchanged trend_of '\u003c2 values' rule yields 'flat' and no escalation label is counted, so status stays 'steady'.\"}],\"private_checks\":[{\"check_id\":\"r1prv1\",\"symbol\":\"summarize_window\",\"input\":[[]],\"expected_values\":[{\"total\":0,\"counts\":{},\"peak\":null,\"status\":\"steady\",\"trend\":\"flat\",\"seen_kinds\":[]}],\"active_from_round\":1,\"active_through_round\":2,\"source_ids\":[\"m04\",\"m05\"],\"behavior\":\"functionality\",\"rationale\":\"m04 explicitly locks the empty-window edge case: total 0, counts {}, peak None, status 'steady', trend 'flat', seen_kinds [].\"},{\"check_id\":\"r1prv2\",\"symbol\":\"summarize_window\",\"input\":[[{\"kind\":\"alarm\",\"value\":99,\"muted\":true},{\"kind\":\"reading\",\"value\":80,\"muted\":true}]],\"expected_values\":[{\"total\":2,\"counts\":{},\"peak\":null,\"status\":\"steady\",\"trend\":\"flat\",\"seen_kinds\":[]}],\"active_from_round\":1,\"active_through_round\":2,\"source_ids\":[\"m02\",\"m04\",\"m05\"],\"behavior\":\"functionality\",\"rationale\":\"Under m02 muted events are excluded from counts, peak, status and trend, so an all-muted window has no escalation label and the still-applicable pre-existing default 'steady' applies; m04 rejects the hallway idea of dropping muted events from total, so total stays 2.\"},{\"check_id\":\"r1prv3\",\"symbol\":\"summarize_window\",\"input\":[[{\"kind\":\"reading\",\"value\":10,\"ts\":1},{\"kind\":\"reading\",\"value\":12,\"ts\":2},{\"kind\":\"reading\",\"value\":90,\"ts\":3,\"muted\":true}]],\"expected_values\":[{\"total\":3,\"counts\":{\"low\":2},\"peak\":12,\"status\":\"steady\",\"trend\":\"flat\",\"seen_kinds\":[\"reading\"]}],\"active_from_round\":1,\"active_through_round\":2,\"source_ids\":[\"m02\",\"m05\"],\"behavior\":\"functionality\",\"rationale\":\"m02 says only unmuted events go to trend_of: unmuted reading values are [10, 12], delta 2, so 'flat'; the trailing muted 90 reading must not flip the trend to 'rising', nor raise peak (stays 12) or counts, while still adding 1 to total.\"},{\"check_id\":\"r1prv4\",\"symbol\":\"summarize_window\",\"input\":[[{\"kind\":\"reading\"},{\"kind\":\"reading\",\"value\":40},{\"kind\":\"alarm\",\"value\":55}]],\"expected_values\":[{\"total\":3,\"counts\":{\"unknown\":1,\"normal\":1,\"warning\":1},\"peak\":40,\"status\":\"watch\",\"trend\":\"flat\",\"seen_kinds\":[\"alarm\",\"reading\"]}],\"active_from_round\":1,\"active_through_round\":2,\"source_ids\":[\"m04\",\"m05\"],\"behavior\":\"functionality\",\"rationale\":\"m04 requires events missing 'value' not to crash peak: the value-less reading keeps its unchanged 'unknown' label, peak comes only from the numeric 40 reading, and the 55 alarm keeps its still-applicable 'warning' label, so the preserved escalation rule yields 'watch'.\"},{\"check_id\":\"r1prv5\",\"symbol\":\"summarize_window\",\"input\":[[{\"kind\":\"alarm\",\"value\":20},{\"kind\":\"alarm\"}]],\"expected_values\":[{\"total\":2,\"counts\":{\"notice\":2},\"peak\":null,\"status\":\"steady\",\"trend\":\"flat\",\"seen_kinds\":[\"alarm\"]}],\"active_from_round\":1,\"active_through_round\":1,\"source_ids\":[\"m01\",\"m05\"],\"behavior\":\"retained\",\"rationale\":\"m01 demands counts and status work exactly as they do now; at this round the unchanged classify_event still maps low-value and value-less alarms to 'notice', and nothing in m01-m04 retires that label, with no escalation so status remains 'steady'.\"},{\"check_id\":\"r1prv6\",\"symbol\":\"summarize_window\",\"input\":[[{\"kind\":\"alarm\",\"value\":95},{\"kind\":\"heartbeat\"}]],\"expected_values\":[{\"total\":2,\"counts\":{\"critical\":1,\"ok\":1},\"peak\":null,\"status\":\"alert\",\"trend\":\"flat\",\"seen_kinds\":[\"alarm\",\"heartbeat\"]}],\"active_from_round\":1,\"active_through_round\":2,\"source_ids\":[\"m01\",\"m05\"],\"behavior\":\"retained\",\"rationale\":\"m01 preserves existing aggregation while adding trend/seen_kinds: a 95 alarm ('critical') plus a heartbeat ('ok') yields status 'alert' under the unchanged escalation, peak None without numeric readings, and 'flat' because trend_of sees no reading values.\"}]}","thinking":"Let me work through round 1 of this project.\n\nThe task: author round 1's packet: reference_patch (replacement for `summarize_window`), public_checks, private_checks.\n\nRound 1 incorporates messages m01–m04:\n- m01: keep total, counts, peak, status; add \"trend\" key = trend_of(events).\n- m02: muted events (with \"muted\": True) still count in \"total\", but excluded from counts, peak, status decision, and trend (pass only unmuted events to trend_of). Unflagged events behave as before; trend stays.\n- m03: add \"seen_kinds\": sorted list of distinct \"kind\" strings among unmuted events ([] when none). Independent.\n- m04: edge cases: empty window returns total 0, counts {}, peak None, status \"steady\", trend \"flat\", seen_kinds []. Missing \"value\" must not crash peak. Muted events stay in total (the hallway idea to drop them from total is rejected).\n\nImportant: round 1 must NOT anticipate future changes (notice retirement, fault kind, limit, info_share, worst, silent status). So status stays \"steady\" for all-muted windows at round 1 (that change comes in round 3). Careful: private checks must not assert \"silent\" in round 1.\n\nAlso important: checks introduced in round 1 that remain active in rounds 2 and 3 need to be consistent with future behavior or be expired. Hmm — \"active_through_round is null (through round 3) or an integer\". Since the labels change in round 2 (\"notice\" retired, replaced by \"info\"), any round-1 check whose expected counts include \"notice\" must expire at round 1 (active_through_round: 1), or avoid \"notice\" labels entirely.\n\nWait, but retained coverage requirements: \"Each round has at least two active private cases for retained behavior (round 1 may derive these from the starting module's specified behavior and initial source instructions).\" Round 1 checks need to be active at round 1. At round 2, retained cases come from round 1 active ones plus round 2. If I expire a notice-involving check at round 1, it's fine.\n\nBut the contract says: \"Avoid overlapping conflicting expected results. A changed expectation gets a new ID; the earlier case's frozen expiry must already make this consistent.\" So for any input whose expected output changes between rounds (due to notice -\u003e info), the earlier check must expire before the later round introduces the conflicting one.\n\nSimplest approach for round 1: design checks whose expected values don't involve \"notice\", or expire any that do at round 1. Actually counts keys only include labels present. If inputs avoid low-value alarms and value-less alarms, no \"notice\" appears. Also status could be \"watch\" from warning or high — alarm with value \u003e= 50 but \u003c 90 gives \"warning\" in round 1 and round 2 as well (unchanged). \"high\" readings unchanged. Critical alarm \u003e= 90 unchanged. So I can craft round-1 checks that stay valid through round 3 if they avoid:\n- \"notice\" labels (use alarm values \u003e= 50 or \u003e= 90, or readings, heartbeats).\n- fault events (round 2 adds them — but round-1 input with kind \"fault\" would return \"unknown\" label; in round 2 it'd return critical/warning/info. So avoid fault in checks that persist; or expire them at round 1... but fault kind wasn't visible in round 1 sources, so I shouldn't even use it).\n- all-muted status: round 1 expects \"steady\" for all-muted windows (since status derivation from counts which are all zero). Round 3 changes to \"silent\". So any round-1 check with all-muted input expecting \"steady\" must expire by round 2 (active_through_round \u003c= 2) to avoid conflict with round 3. I can set expiry 2 and round 3 introduces a new check with same input expecting \"silent\". Or just set expiry 1 and not worry. But better to have coverage. The instruction says round 3 will need retirement checks for the \"steady\"-in-all-muted rule. That's round 3's problem. For round 1, I'll include an all-muted check with status \"steady\", trend \"flat\", counts {}, seen_kinds [], peak None, total N, and expire it at round 2 (active_through_round: 2), since round 3 supersedes it.\n\nHmm, but wait: in round 2, does all-muted still give \"steady\"? Round 2 only changes classify_event. Yes. So active_through_round 2 is fine.\n\nAlso info_share/worst keys are added in round 3 — the expected_values compare whole dict with strict JSON equality. So round-1 checks with full-dict expected values active through round 3 would fail in round 3 because the dict gains extra keys! Expected_values is compared using strict JSON equality against the function output. The output is a dict. In round 3, summarize_window returns extra keys info_share and worst. So any round-1 summarize_window check that remains active into round 3 would need expected dicts including those keys — but that would anticipate round 3. Hence all round-1 summarize_window full-dict checks MUST expire by round 2 at latest (active_through_round \u003c= 2), so that round 3 can introduce new full-dict checks.\n\nHmm wait, is that right? Strict JSON equality — presumably deep equality between actual output and expected value. If expected lacks \"info_share\" but output has it, they're not equal. Yes. So round-1 checks that survive to round 3 must have expected values matching round-3 outputs, which we cannot know at round 1 without anticipating. Therefore expire every summarize_window full-dict round-1 check at round 2 at latest.\n\nBut then at round 3, retained private coverage must come from round-2 and round-3 checks. That's fine — contract says \"Coverage may reuse earlier active cases exactly as the contract permits\", but doesn't force round-1 cases to persist.\n\nCould I design round-1 checks whose expected value is not the whole dict? No — expected_values elements are compared against the function's return; they must be the full return value. So yes, full dict.\n\nSo plan: round-1 checks (both public and private) on summarize_window with active_through_round = 1 or 2. Choose which persist to round 2: those whose outputs don't change under round 2's classify_event changes. Labels produced by round-1 classifier: critical, warning, notice, high, low, normal, ok, unknown. Round 2 retires notice -\u003e info. So a round-1 check whose counts/status involve \"notice\" must expire at round 1. Others can expire at round 2 (they'd otherwise conflict in round 3 due to new keys).\n\nLet me also think: should some round-1 checks persist through round 3? It's only possible for trend_of checks! trend_of is an existing function; round 1 doesn't change it, but I can add checks on trend_of symbol? They're allowed: symbol is an existing function. trend_of behavior never changes across rounds. A round-1 trend_of check could be active through round 3 and serve as retained coverage. That helps satisfy \"at each round, at least two active private cases must cover retained behavior\" — including cases introduced earlier that remain active.\n\nActually retained coverage at round 2 needs ≥2 active private retained cases. Round-2 introduces its own; round-1 checks that remain active also count (\"Coverage is derived from introduction and current round as well as the tag\"). I'll tag round-1 checks on unchanged behavior as \"functionality\" or \"retained\"? The behavior tag: \"functionality\" for new features, \"retained\" for preserved behavior, \"retirement\" for retired. For round 1: trend key = new functionality; muted handling = new functionality; seen_kinds = new; empty window edge = functionality (locking in). Cases asserting that unmuted events behave exactly as before (e.g., basic counts/peak/status preserved) = retained.\n\nLet me now write the round-1 reference patch:\n\n```python\ndef summarize_window(events):\n    counts = {}\n    peak = None\n    total = 0\n    unmuted = []\n    for event in events:\n        total += 1\n        if event.get(\"muted\"):\n            continue\n        unmuted.append(event)\n        label = classify_event(event)\n        counts[label] = counts.get(label, 0) + 1\n        if event.get(\"kind\") == \"reading\":\n            value = event.get(\"value\")\n            if value is not None and (peak is None or value \u003e peak):\n                peak = value\n    status = \"steady\"\n    if counts.get(\"critical\"):\n        status = \"alert\"\n    elif counts.get(\"warning\") or counts.get(\"high\"):\n        status = \"watch\"\n    seen_kinds = sorted({event.get(\"kind\") for event in unmuted})\n    return {\"total\": total, \"counts\": counts, \"peak\": peak,\n            \"status\": status, \"trend\": trend_of(unmuted), \"seen_kinds\": seen_kinds}\n```\n\nWait — seen_kinds: \"distinct 'kind' strings among unmuted events\". If an event lacks \"kind\"? Events are dicts like {\"kind\": ...}. Assume kind always present. Using event.get(\"kind\") could include None; sorted with None and strings would error in Python 3. Safer: iterate and collect only strings? The spec says \"distinct kind strings\". I could do:\n\n```python\n    seen = []\n    for event in unmuted:\n        kind = event.get(\"kind\")\n        if isinstance(kind, str) and kind not in seen:\n            seen.append(kind)\n    seen_kinds = sorted(seen)\n```\n\nHmm, but keep it compact. Given events always have kind strings per spec, `sorted({e.get(\"kind\") for e in unmuted})` works, but if kind missing it puts None. To be safe, use the isinstance filter. Keep within token budget. Let me write compactly.\n\nAlso note m02: \"pass only the unmuted events to trend_of\". trend_of filters readings with values anyway.\n\nRound-1 patch must only replace summarize_window, not touch classify_event or trend_of. \"Produce only a replacement for this round's existing target function.\" Good.\n\nNow checks. Round 1: check IDs unique across project, e.g., \"r1-pub-01\" etc. Let me use IDs like \"c01\"... Any format. I'll use \"staged04-r1-pub-01\"? Keep compact: \"r1p1\", \"r1p2\" public; \"r1s1\"... Let me use descriptive: \"pub1a\", \"pub1b\", \"pvt1a\"... IDs must be unique across the project; prefixing with r1 ensures that.\n\nPublic checks (introduced round 1): visible to workers. Should cover the new functionality: trend key, muted handling, seen_kinds. Maybe 2-3 public checks.\n\nPrivate checks: at least 2, at least one functionality. Include retained-behavior derivable cases. Also include boundary/conditional interactions (muted + trend interplay, all-muted, empty, missing value, muted shouldn't affect peak, etc.). Also a notice-involving case expiring at round 1 to test the pre-existing classifier integration (retained through round 1 only).\n\nBut note: expiry intervals must be set correctly given the future schedule. Round-1 summarize_window checks: any that remain active into round 3 would need info_share/worst keys — impossible. So max expiry 2. And those involving \"notice\" must expire at 1. And all-muted \"steady\" must expire at 2 (round 3 supersedes with \"silent\" — and round 3 will introduce its own check).\n\nAlso think about potential conflicts: round 2 introduces checks on classify_event mostly (target is classify_event). Round 2 may also add summarize_window checks? Target is classify_event, but checks can be on any existing symbol. Doesn't matter for round 1 authoring; I only author round 1 now. But I must ensure round-1 checks that persist to round 2 remain CORRECT under round 2's reference (classify_event updated). Since summarize_window calls classify_event, round-1 outputs computed with the round-1 classifier must match round-2 classifier outputs for those inputs. Avoid notice-producing inputs in checks with expiry 2. Also avoid... round 2 doesn't change readings/heartbeat/alarm thresholds except notice-\u003einfo. Alarm value \u003e= 50 warning unchanged; \u003e= 90 critical unchanged; below 50 becomes \"info\" in round 2 but \"notice\" in round 1 — avoid such inputs for expiry-2 checks (or expire at 1). Alarms with value None similarly. Unknown kinds unchanged (\"unknown\"). So expiry-2 checks use: critical alarms, warning alarms, readings (high/low/normal), heartbeats, unknown kinds — outputs identical in rounds 1 and 2.\n\nLet me now design the cases.\n\nPublic checks:\n\n1. \"r1-pub-trend\" — functionality: basic window with rising trend, no muted.\nInput: [{\"kind\": \"reading\", \"value\": 10, \"ts\": 1}, {\"kind\": \"reading\", \"value\": 30, \"ts\": 2}]\nclassify: 10 \u003c= 25 -\u003e \"low\"; 30 normal (25\u003c30\u003c75) -\u003e \"normal\". counts {\"low\":1,\"normal\":1}. peak 30. total 2. status: no critical/warning/high -\u003e \"steady\". trend: values [10,30], delta 20 \u003e= 5 -\u003e \"rising\". seen_kinds [\"reading\"].\nExpected: {\"total\":2,\"counts\":{\"low\":1,\"normal\":1},\"peak\":30,\"status\":\"steady\",\"trend\":\"rising\",\"seen_kinds\":[\"reading\"]}\nActive round 1 through 2 (no notice, outputs unchanged in round 2). Expire 2 to avoid round-3 conflict.\nsource_ids: m01, m05? m05 is the request. Include \"m01\",\"m05\". Actually request IDs are allowed: \"source_ids are nonempty unique source/request IDs visible at introduction.\" Include m01 and m05.\n\n2. \"r1-pub-muted\" — functionality: muted exclusion.\nInput: [{\"kind\":\"alarm\",\"value\":95,\"muted\":True}, {\"kind\":\"reading\",\"value\":60,\"ts\":2}, {\"kind\":\"heartbeat\"}]\ntotal 3. counts: reading 60 -\u003e normal; heartbeat -\u003e ok =\u003e {\"normal\":1,\"ok\":1}. peak 60. status steady. trend: single reading -\u003e \"flat\". seen_kinds sorted distinct: [\"heartbeat\",\"reading\"].\nsource_ids: m01, m02, m03, m05.\nActive 1..2.\n\nPrivate checks (round 1, at least 2; I'll add ~5):\n\n1. \"r1-pvt-empty\" — functionality/boundary: empty window.\nInput: [] -\u003e {\"total\":0,\"counts\":{},\"peak\":None,\"status\":\"steady\",\"trend\":\"flat\",\"seen_kinds\":[]}.\nsource_ids m04, m05. Active 1..2? Round 2 unchanged (no events =\u003e unaffected by classifier). Round 3 adds keys -\u003e conflict. So expiry 2.\n\n2. \"r1-pvt-allmuted\" — functionality/boundary: all muted window stays steady (round 1). This is the round-1 expectation which will be superseded in round 3.\nInput: [{\"kind\":\"alarm\",\"value\":99,\"muted\":True},{\"kind\":\"reading\",\"value\":80,\"muted\":True}]\ntotal 2, counts {}, peak None, status \"steady\", trend \"flat\", seen_kinds [].\nActive 1..2. source_ids m02, m04, m05. Behavior: functionality (locked-in edge). Rationale notes muted excluded from everything except total; status falls back to steady per unmodified escalation; the dropping-from-total idea rejected (m04).\n\n3. \"r1-pvt-muted-trend\" — conditional interaction: muted readings excluded from trend so trend is computed only from unmuted.\nInput: [{\"kind\":\"reading\",\"value\":10}, {\"kind\":\"reading\",\"value\":50,\"muted\":True}, {\"kind\":\"reading\",\"value\":8}]\nUnmuted readings values: [10, 8] -\u003e delta -2 -\u003e \"flat\". If muted were included: [10,50,8] delta -2 also flat — bad discriminator. Choose values where inclusion changes result: unmuted [10, 12] delta 2 -\u003e flat; with muted 50 included: [10,50,12] delta 2 still flat. Hmm delta uses first and last only! trend = last - first. So make the muted event the last reading: unmuted [10, 12] -\u003e delta 2 flat; including muted final value 90 -\u003e delta 80 rising. Also peak: muted 90 must not affect peak -\u003e peak 12. counts: 10 -\u003e low, 12 -\u003e low =\u003e {\"low\":2}. status steady. seen_kinds [\"reading\"]. total 3.\nInput: [{\"kind\":\"reading\",\"value\":10,\"ts\":1},{\"kind\":\"reading\",\"value\":12,\"ts\":2},{\"kind\":\"reading\",\"value\":90,\"ts\":3,\"muted\":True}]\nExpected: total 3, counts {\"low\":2}, peak 12, status \"steady\", trend \"flat\", seen_kinds [\"reading\"].\nBehavior functionality. source_ids m02, m05.\nActive 1..2 (no notice; round 2 same outputs).\n\n4. \"r1-pvt-missing-value\" — retained/boundary: reading missing value doesn't crash peak; contributes count label.\nInput: [{\"kind\":\"reading\"},{\"kind\":\"reading\",\"value\":40},{\"kind\":\"alarm\",\"value\":55}]\nclassify({\"kind\":\"reading\"}) -\u003e value None -\u003e \"unknown\". reading 40 -\u003e normal. alarm 55 -\u003e warning.\ncounts {\"unknown\":1,\"normal\":1,\"warning\":1}. peak 40. status \"watch\" (warning). trend: reading values [40] -\u003e flat. seen_kinds [\"alarm\",\"reading\"]. total 3.\nsource_ids m04, m05. Behavior functionality? It's about locked edges + preserved aggregation. The missing-value edge is from m04. Status watch from warning is pre-existing retained. I'll tag \"functionality\" since it locks m04 edge... Actually \"behavior\" describes what the case tests. The missing-value-no-crash is locked-in edge (functionality per m04). Fine.\nActive 1..2. Warning unchanged in round 2. Good.\n\n5. \"r1-pvt-notice\" — retained, expiry 1: verifies pre-round-1 labeling integration including notice (retired next round). Input with low-value alarm.\nInput: [{\"kind\":\"alarm\",\"value\":20},{\"kind\":\"alarm\"}]\nRound 1: alarm 20 -\u003e \"notice\"; alarm without value -\u003e \"notice\". counts {\"notice\":2}, total 2, peak None, status \"steady\", trend \"flat\", seen_kinds [\"alarm\"].\nActive 1..1 (expires because round 2 retires notice). Behavior: \"retained\" (tests st