d added.\n\nSo round-1 private: empty (functionality), allmuted (functionality), muted-trend (functionality), missing-value (functionality), notice (retained, exp 1), alert-status (retained, exp 2). That's ≥2 private, ≥1 functionality, ≥2 retained active at round 1. \n\nAt round 2, active round-1 private checks: all except notice one. They must remain correct under round-2 reference — verify none produce \"notice\" and none use fault/limit. Inputs: none have low alarms or kind fault. Good.\n\nRound 1 doesn't need retirement cases (only rounds 2 and 3 do).\n\nNow also confirm no anticipated behavior: no check uses \"info\", \"fault\", \"silent\", \"info_share\", \"worst\". Good.\n\nNow check strict JSON equality with number types: peak 60 (int) vs 60.0 — JSON strict equality might consider 60 == 60.0? \"compared using the existing strict JSON equality\" — ambiguous; keep values as ints where input ints, and Python returns ints for int inputs. Fine.\n\nAlso trend_of receives only unmuted events; ensure reference passes original full event dicts (fine).\n\nseen_kinds sorted: [\"alarm\",\"heartbeat\"] sorted alphabetically: \"alarm\" \u003c \"heartbeat\". Yes. [\"heartbeat\",\"reading\"]: h \u003c r. Good.\n\nNow compute all expected values precisely.\n\nPublic 1 input: [{\"kind\":\"reading\",\"value\":10,\"ts\":1},{\"kind\":\"reading\",\"value\":30,\"ts\":2}]\n- total 2\n- classify(10): reading, 10 \u003c= 25 -\u003e \"low\"; classify(30): 25\u003c30\u003c75 -\u003e \"normal\"\n- counts {\"low\":1,\"normal\":1}\n- peak: 10 then 30 -\u003e 30\n- status: no critical; warning? no; high? no -\u003e \"steady\"\n- trend: [10,30] delta 20 -\u003e \"rising\"\n- seen_kinds [\"reading\"]\nExpected dict: {\"total\":2,\"counts\":{\"low\":1,\"normal\":1},\"peak\":30,\"status\":\"steady\",\"trend\":\"rising\",\"seen_kinds\":[\"reading\"]}\n\nPublic 2 input: [{\"kind\":\"alarm\",\"value\":95,\"muted\":True},{\"kind\":\"reading\",\"value\":60,\"ts\":2},{\"kind\":\"heartbeat\"}]\n- tot